394:
The public art advisory committee may adopt specific criteria for review of proposed permanent public art projects and guidelines therefore.
(1) The initial guidelines and criteria for review of proposed permanent public art projects shall be as follows:
a. Relevance of the work to the City of Valdosta, its values, culture and people;
b. Technical suitability of the work for outdoor display, including its maintenance and conservation requirements; and
c. Relationship of the work to the site, especially how it serves to activate or enhance the public space.
(2) In general, a proposed work of public art will not be accepted if it is not original sculpture or painting. If accepted artwork is a multiple, it will only be accepted if it is a limited edition. Generally reproductions; unlimited editions; decorative, ornamental and functional elements of architecture; directional elements, such as super graphics, signage and color-coding usually are not considered artwork unless done by an artist.
a. Acceptable media include:
1. Sculpture: in the round, bas-relief, mobile, fountain, kinetic, electronic;
2. Mosaic: ceramic, glass, metal, wood, mixed media;
3. Fountains or water elements;
4. Crafts: Clay fiber, textiles, wood, metal, plastics, stained glass;
5. Mixed media;
6. Earthworks and environmental artworks;
7. Decorative, ornamental or functional elements designed by an artist; and
8. Murals and paintings.
b. Unacceptable criteria: The artwork does not meet professional standards for public art due to one or more of the following:
1. Faults of design or workmanship which will either pose a public safety hazard or diminish the value of the work.
2. The possibility that the artwork is fraudulent or not authentic.
3. The artwork appears to be of inferior quality relative to the quality of other works in the city's collection.
c. The public art advisory committee will consider the following questions and criteria when conducting its review:
1. Aesthetic quality and artistic merit;
2. Placement and sitting;
3. Fabrication, handling and installation;
4. Maintenance requirements;
5. Conservation and preservation;
6. Liability and safety; and
7. Community.
(Ord. No. 2010-9, § 1, 2-25-2010)

