(a) A determination shall be made for a five-year period of what capital improvements are needed or desirable, arranged in order of priority. Both uncompleted projects currently underway and newly proposed projects shall be included.
(b) The financial resources such as general obligation bonds and revenue bonds shall be analyzed to determine the funds which should be available or can be provided within safe limits for construction or completion of capital improvements during a five-year period.
(c) The first year of the five-year schedule, called the capital budget, is considered the capital budget for the current fiscal year and shall include only funded projects.
(d) Those new projects which are tentatively selected for construction as well as ongoing projects to be completed shall be scheduled over the remaining four-year period in accordance with their need and desirability and in direct relation to the funds available.
(e) At the end of each year the capital improvement program shall be reviewed. Any projects that are no longer justified or needed shall be eliminated; others may be shifted in position in the program in accordance with changing needs. An additional year shall be added to replace the year just completed. Projects included in the budget for the current year shall again appear in the first year of the five-year program; and new and additional projects shall fall somewhere within the remaining four years based upon consideration or priority, feasibility, and availability of funds.
(f) The criteria to be used in analyzing proposed projects and their relative importance and priority shall include, without limitation, the following:
(1) Protection of life;
(2) Maintenance of public health;
(3) Protection of property;
(4) Maintenance of physical property;
(5) Provision of public services;
(6) Replacement of obsolete facilities;
(7) Reduction of operating costs;
(8) Public convenience and comfort; and
(9) Enhancement of community appearance.
(Code 1976, § 2-5035)

